{"parentUuid":"cdcdc499-5c10-4485-bcc7-6cc58c188250","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkBapXcXbfRZUvBwYJrP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The line isn't drawn by how much of the work AI does. It's drawn by who makes the decisions and who can defend them. A thesis certifies that you can do research: frame a problem, judge evidence, design something, and argue for it. AI can do the labour. It can't be the one deciding, and you have to be able to explain every choice without it.\n\nA simple test for each piece: **could you defend it in your defense with no AI in the room?** If yes, how it was produced matters much less.\n\n**Your plan, phase by phase:**\n\n| Phase | Your split | Is it fine? | What has to be yours |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Problem | You | Yes | Already yours |\n| Research | AI searches, you approve | Yes, with one condition | Each include/exclude decision and its reason, recorded. Read in full the papers your argument depends on; for those, AI summaries aren't enough. |\n| Design | You propose, AI writes and critiques | Yes | The design choices and the rejected alternatives. That's the \"Artifact description\" chapter. |\n| Develop | AI writes code and tests | Yes, with one condition | You define the evaluation metrics before you see results, and you understand the code well enough to explain why a result came out the way it did. |\n| Communicate | AI drafts, ideas are yours | **Riskiest part** | See below |\n\n**Writing is where you could actually get in trouble.** Many universities treat the text itself as part of what's assessed. \"The ideas are in my head\" doesn't count if the argument structure and the claims on the page came from the model. A safer split: you write the outline and the key claims, even roughly. AI turns that into prose. You revise until every sentence says what you mean. Check your faculty's rules and ask your advisor directly. That conversation protects you more than anything else here.\n\n**\"I know what I want to prove\" is also a risk.** It's a methodological risk, not an AI one, and AI makes it worse. Two parts of your own method exist to guard against it:\n- The SLS protocol is written first so that selection doesn't bend toward what you hoped to find. An AI told what you want to prove will happily find support for it.\n- DSRM evaluation has to be able to fail. Your H1–H3 are written as hypotheses, which is good. Keep them falsifiable, and record evaluations that go against you.\n\nSo when you use AI for research, ask it for counter-evidence too, like the Go message-passing bugs result you already cite.\n\n**How to make it visible**, which fits how your repo already works:\n1. Add a section to the SLS protocol and the DSRM README stating which tasks AI does and which decisions you make.\n2. Keep an `ai-log.md` (or a field in each step) recording the tool, the task, and what you accepted, changed or rejected. It's the same traceability idea as your IDs.\n3. In the thesis's Method chapter, add a short paragraph disclosing AI use. More and more venues expect this, and disclosed use is rarely a problem.\n\nIf you want, I can draft the protocol section and the log template in your repo's style."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":160,"cache_read_input_tokens":44326,"output_tokens":1449,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":466},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":160,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1449,"cache_read_input_tokens":44326,"cache_creation_input_tokens":160,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":160},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformatio