{"parentUuid":"980b6003-9442-49b9-8d7a-e902e7b56d06","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkDBUtkzbntcZnMahL6Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8537,"cache_read_input_tokens":45978,"output_tokens":1693,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":140},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8537,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1693,"cache_read_input_tokens":45978,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8537,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8537},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":9448,"serverClassifierRequest":"37f948fa-3f3b-43bd-9f39-867e7a7fcc1b","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfkDBUa9rSC7Y6uWDd3PD","type":"assistant","uuid":"41e9efa0-0106-4cb4-894d-93f10edbedac","timestamp":"2026-10-06T02:40:53.953Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis/research","sessionId":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"41e9efa0-0106-4cb4-894d-93f10edbedac","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkDBUtkzbntcZnMahL6Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here are both methods as step lists, in the order your `research/README.md` connects them. Section numbers match the `step.md` files.\n\n## SLS: Systematic Literature Study\n\n**1. Protocol** (written before any searching)\n1. State what the review is about, starting from the DSRM observation (dsrm 1.1).\n2. List existing reviews on the topic and why they aren't enough.\n3. Write the review questions (SQ1, SQ2, …) and why each matters.\n4. Define the inclusion (I1…) and exclusion (X1…) criteria.\n5. Write the quality checklist (Q1…) with its scale (yes / partly / no).\n6. Define the data extraction form: the fields recorded for every paper.\n7. Log any later change to the protocol, with the date and reason.\n\n**2. Search (snowballing)**\n1. Build the start set from a search string or source.\n2. Screen each paper against I/X and give included ones an ID (P1, P2, …).\n3. For each paper included in the previous round, check its references (backward) and the papers citing it (forward).\n4. Screen the candidates and record the newly included IDs.\n5. Repeat steps 3–4. **Stop** when a round includes no new papers.\n\n**3. Reading** (for each included paper)\n1. First pass: the five Cs (category, context, correctness, contributions, clarity).\n2. Read further (pass 2 or 3) only if you need more from the paper.\n3. Score it on Q1…\n4. Fill in the extraction fields, with page numbers.\n5. Note the concepts found, with page numbers.\n\n**4. Synthesis**\n1. Define each concept (C1…) and note where it first appeared.\n2. Fill the concept matrix (papers × concepts, with page numbers).\n3. For each concept, compare what the papers say.\n\n**5. Gap**\n1. Answer each SQ, citing papers.\n2. State the gap: concepts or combinations no paper covers.\n3. Describe current solutions and how far they get.\n4. Explain why it hasn't been done, or why earlier attempts failed.\n5. List the review's limitations and possible biases.\n\n## DSRM: Design Science Research Methodology\n\n**1. Problem** (entry point)\n1. State the observation as a gap (1.1). Its output starts SLS step 1.\n2. Write the motivation and the scope, using SLS 5.\n3. Explain why earlier attempts failed and state the hypotheses (H1–H3).\n4. Identify the stakeholders and their goals.\n5. Write exactly one design problem (Wieringa's template).\n6. Write the knowledge questions (RQ…) with their Shaw type and the evidence that would answer them. Each must be answerable with \"no\".\n\n**2. Objectives**\n1. Derive objectives (O1…) from the problem and from what SLS 5 shows is feasible.\n2. Link each one to an RQ and mark it quantitative or qualitative.\n3. Say how each one will be checked.\n\n**3. Design** (repeats every iteration)\n1. Pick the cheapest layer not yet built: paper design → simulator → formal spec (optional) → hardware (optional).\n2. Build it, using theory from SLS 4.\n3. Record each design decision: options considered, the choice, and why.\n4. Demonstrate and evaluate the layer before starting the next.\n\n**4. Demonstration**\n1. Plan the demonstrations (D1…): problem instance, layer, activity, and which objectives they feed.\n2. Run them and log each run: what happened, whether it solved the instance, and observations for evaluation.\n\n**5. Evaluation**\n1. For each objective, choose a Shaw validation type and justify it.\n2. Compare the demonstration results with the objectives and log each one (E…): met or not met.\n3. Decide what's next: go to 6, back to 2, or back to 3, with the reason.\n4. Record the threats to validity (conclusion, internal, construct, external) and what you did about each.\n\n**6. Communicate**\n1. Write the thesis chapters from the stable steps, following the outline.\n2. Mark a chapter stale when its source has changed since it was last synced.\n3. State the contribution type (improvement / invention / exaptation / routine design) and why.\n4. Go back to 2 or 3 if writing exposes a hole.\n\n## Overall order\nDSRM 1.1 → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1 (rest) → 2 → (3 → 4 → 5)* → 6\n\nSLS 5 feeds DSRM 1 and 2, and SLS 4 feeds DSRM 3.\n\nRight now DSRM step 1 is mostly filled in, ahead of SLS, but the RQ3 metrics and baselines are still empty. Everything else is still a template."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8537,"cache_read_input_tokens":45978,"output_tokens":1693,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":140},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8537,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1693,"cache_read_input_tokens":45978,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8537,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8537},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"37f948fa-3f3b-43bd-9f39-867e7a7fcc1b","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfkDBUa9rSC7Y6uWDd3PD","type":"assistant","uuid":"e5012f7e-808b-4a23-9673-1daa0933da71","timestamp":"2026-10-06T02:41:03.589Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis/research","sessionId":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
