{"parentUuid":"070224d7-2f39-4814-9326-193c71120047","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkEiw3M6m1x1CPAeuKCJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":107,"cache_read_input_tokens":57576,"output_tokens":3292,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1359},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":107,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3292,"cache_read_input_tokens":57576,"cache_creation_input_tokens":107,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":107},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":19217,"serverClassifierRequest":"55dbcc92-065e-4276-afd1-009c592517ab","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfkEivEUdVQCgWGoduaH2","type":"assistant","uuid":"c9778df2-a747-46d2-98d7-2392858db687","timestamp":"2026-10-06T03:01:17.299Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis","sessionId":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c9778df2-a747-46d2-98d7-2392858db687","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkEiw3M6m1x1CPAeuKCJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Below is the table for every step. I've split the old `builder` into a **designer**, which decides the design with you, and a **builder**, which produces the chosen layer in whatever form that layer takes. I also added two agents that run across all steps, because AI-written research fails in two predictable ways: fabricated citations and confirmation bias.\n\n## Per step\n\n| Step | Agent | Reads | Does | Writes (proposed) | You decide |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **SLS 1** Protocol | `protocol-critic` | dsrm 1.1, your protocol draft | Finds existing reviews; checks that each SQ is answerable, that I/X criteria can be applied without ambiguity, and that the extraction form covers every SQ | Comments, 1.1 list of existing reviews, 1.6 log entry | SQs, criteria, checklist, form. The protocol is your guard against bias, so you write it. |\n| **SLS 2** Search | `snowballer` | Protocol, 2.2 last round | Proposes the start set; for each round, fetches references and citations and pre-screens each candidate with the criterion it matches | 2.1/2.2 rows marked *proposed* | Every include/exclude |\n| **SLS 3** Reading | `reader` | Paper, protocol 1.4–1.5 | First pass (five Cs), Q scores, extraction fields and concepts, each with page numbers | Draft record for each P | Corrects records; reads the papers your argument depends on in full |\n| **SLS 4** Synthesis | `synthesizer` | All records | Proposes concepts (C…), fills the matrix, drafts a comparison for each concept | 4.1–4.3 drafts | Merges/splits concepts, accepts the comparisons |\n| **SLS 5** Gap | `synthesizer` | Matrix | Drafts SQ answers with citations, the uncovered combinations, the history and the limitations | 5.1–5.4 drafts | **States the gap** |\n| **DSRM 1** Problem | `skeptic` | 1.x, SLS 5 | Attacks the motivation and H1–H3, looks for counter-evidence (like the Go result), checks that each RQ can be answered \"no\" | Comments | Problem, design problem, RQs |\n| **DSRM 2** Objectives | `skeptic` | 1.5, SLS 5 | Checks each O: traces to an RQ, is checkable, is feasible given current solutions | Comments | Objectives and how each is checked, **including RQ3 metrics and baselines** |\n| **DSRM 3** Design | `designer` | Objectives, SLS 4 theory, decisions so far | For each open decision: generates options, compares trade-offs against objectives and prior art (e.g. 432's mistakes), critiques your proposals | Decision rows: options + analysis | **Chosen option and why** |\n| | `builder` | Decisions, current layer | Produces the layer in its form (see below) and checks it with that layer's kind of check | The layer itself + its checks | Reviews and accepts the layer; you must be able to explain it |\n| **DSRM 4** Demonstration | `demonstrator` | Layer, 4.1 plan | Writes problem instances (actor programs, OS-function scenarios), runs them or traces them by hand on paper layers | D log entries | The demonstration plan; confirms \"solved\" |\n| **DSRM 5** Evaluation | `evaluator` | O, D log, metrics | Runs the metrics against baselines, drafts E entries, proposes threats to validity | 5.2–5.3 drafts | Met / not met, **where the next iteration goes** |\n| | `skeptic` | E entries | Attacks results: weak baselines, construct validity, cherry-picked instances | Comments | What to address |\n| **DSRM 6** Communicate | `drafter` | Stable steps, your outline + claims | Turns your outline and claims into chapter prose; marks stale chapters | Chapter drafts | Outline, claims, final wording, contribution type |\n| | `examiner` | Chapter drafts | Plays your committee: asks the defense questions you'll face | Question list | Answers them yourself |\n\n## What the builder produces at each layer\n\n| Layer | Form | Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Paper design | Spec document: instruction set, message format, actor lifecycle, diagrams | Consistency review, worked traces by hand |\n| 2. Simulator | Code + tests | Test suite, runs actor programs |\n| 3. Formal spec | TLA+ / Alloy / Coq model | Model checker or proof |\n| 4. Hardware | HDL (Verilog/VHDL/Chisel), FPGA build | HDL simulation, synthesis reports, board runs |\n\nBeyond the layers themselves, the builder also produces benchmark programs, the toolchain (assembler, language front end) and figures. So the builder's job is defined by the layer, not by the fact that it's code.\n\n## Across all steps\n\n| Agent / tool | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `citation-checker` | Checks that each cited claim actually appears in the source at the cited page. AI's most common failure in research writing is a real paper cited for something it never says. |\n| `skeptic` | Above. It can be called from any step. |\n| `just check` (script, not an agent) | Traceability, the ID rule, stale chapters, the snowballing stop rule |\n| AI log | Each agent run appends: agent, step, what was proposed, and what you accepted, changed or rejected |\n\nThat makes 11 agents. You can start with fewer, because the step order means you only need `protocol-critic`, `snowballer`, `reader` and `citation-checker` for the next few weeks.\n\nShould I write this into the repo as `research/agents.md` and link it from `research/README.md`?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":107,"cache_read_input_tokens":57576,"output_tokens":3292,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1359},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":107,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3292,"cache_read_input_tokens":57576,"cache_creation_input_tokens":107,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tok