{"parentUuid":"2c43aaeb-e26f-477c-a792-8c91d0db0dad","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis/research/ai/entry-points.md","snippet":"1\t# Entry points\n2\t\n3\tPeffers et al. define four entry points [1]. The DSRM sequence is the same in all four; only the starting activity changes. The `thesis` skill asks for one (`skills.md`).\n4\t\n5\t| Entry point [1] | Starts at | Trigger | Route |\n6\t|---|---|---|---|\n7\t| Problem-centred initiation | DSRM 1 | An observed problem | Step 0 (`0-start/`: my observation, problem, RQs) → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1 built from step 0 and the SLS → 2 → 3–5 loop → 6. **This thesis.** `flow-sls.md` and `flow-dsrm.md` are this route. |\n8\t| Objective-centred solution | DSRM 2 | A need that a better artifact could meet | Step 0 (`0-start/`: my draft objectives) → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1 written, 2 revised → 3–5 loop → 6 |\n9\t| Design and development-centred initiation | DSRM 3 | An existing artifact not yet thought through as a solution to this problem | Step 0 (`0-start/`: the artifact, described) → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1–2 written for the problem it solves → 3–5 loop → 6 |\n10\t| Client/context initiated | DSRM 4 | A practical solution observed to work | Step 0 (`0-start/`: the observed solution, described) → SLS 1–5 → work backward through DSRM 3, 2, 1 to apply rigor retroactively [1] → 5 → 6 |\n11\t\n12\tMy rules for all four (not part of Peffers):\n13\t- Step 0 is always my input in `0-start/`, frozen before the SLS starts (`kickstart.md`). What it holds depends on the entry point.\n14\t- The SLS always runs right after step 0, because DSRM 1–3 need it (`research/README.md`).\n15\t- The kickstart safeguards apply whatever the entry point.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## References\n20\t1. Peffers et al. (2007). *A Design Science Research Methodology for Information Systems Research*. Journal of Management Information Systems 24(3)."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"158adf80-3421-4f4e-af17-1f992d56e13f","timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:18:59.392Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis/research/ai/entry-points.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t# Entry points\n2\t\n3\tPeffers et al. define four entry points [1]. The DSRM sequence is the same in all four; only the starting activity changes. The `thesis` skill asks for one (`skills.md`).\n4\t\n5\t| Entry point [1] | Starts at | Trigger | Route |\n6\t|---|---|---|---|\n7\t| Problem-centred initiation | DSRM 1 | An observed problem | Step 0 (`0-start/`: my observation, problem, RQs) → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1 built from step 0 and the SLS → 2 → 3–5 loop → 6. **This thesis.** `flow-sls.md` and `flow-dsrm.md` are this route. |\n8\t| Objective-centred solution | DSRM 2 | A need that a better artifact could meet | Step 0 (`0-start/`: my draft objectives) → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1 written, 2 revised → 3–5 loop → 6 |\n9\t| Design and development-centred initiation | DSRM 3 | An existing artifact not yet thought through as a solution to this problem | Step 0 (`0-start/`: the artifact, described) → SLS 1–5 → DSRM 1–2 written for the problem it solves → 3–5 loop → 6 |\n10\t| Client/context initiated | DSRM 4 | A practical solution observed to work | Step 0 (`0-start/`: the observed solution, described) → SLS 1–5 → work backward through DSRM 3, 2, 1 to apply rigor retroactively [1] → 5 → 6 |\n11\t\n12\tMy rules for all four (not part of Peffers):\n13\t- Step 0 is always my input in `0-start/`, frozen before the SLS starts (`kickstart.md`). What it holds depends on the entry point.\n14\t- The SLS always runs right after step 0, because DSRM 1–3 need it (`research/README.md`).\n15\t- The kickstart safeguards apply whatever the entry point.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## References\n20\t1. Peffers et al. (2007). *A Design Science Research Methodology for Information Systems Research*. Journal of Management Information Systems 24(3).\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis/research/ai","sessionId":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
