` | yes | The observation as a gap: what is true in one domain and not in another |","+| `seeds.md` | no | References I already know. Seeds for the start set (row 2a), not results. |","+| `notes.md` | no | Anything I already think about the problem, as I wrote it. Input for DSRM 1, not a commitment. |"," "," | # | Who | Input | Output | Gate |"," |---|---|---|---|---|","-| **0** | Me | — | `0-start/*.md`, all `provisional` | **Start.** Frozen when row 1a runs. |","+| **0** | Me | — | `0-start/*.md` | **Start.** Frozen when row 1a runs. |"," ","+Everything else about the problem (motivation, design problem, RQs, hypotheses, stances, stakeholders) is built in DSRM 1, after the SLS, from the observation and the literature (rows 6a–6d, `flow-dsrm.md`). Peffers et al. build the problem from knowledge of the state of the problem [2]; step 0 comes before that knowledge.","+"," ## Rules","-- **Step 0 is never edited after it is frozen.** Only `status` changes. If the SLS shows something in it is wrong, the corrected version is a new file with a new ID in `dsrm/1-problem/`, and the old one is `retired` (the ID rule, `dsrm/README.md`).","-- **`claims.md` and `seeds.md` are what the AI checks.** The other files are my intent; the AI attacks them (`skeptic`), but they change only if I change them.","+- **Step 0 is never edited after it is frozen.** It records where the work started.","+- **`notes.md` is checked, not trusted.** DSRM 1 marks each of its claims confirmed, to revise or contradicted by the literature (row 6a)."," "," ## Safeguards","-Step 0 already argues for a conclusion, so:","-- **Protocol bias:** SQs drawn only from my hypotheses would only look for support. `skeptic` adds SQs that look for evidence against them (row 1b, `flow-sls.md`).","+- **Protocol bias:** if `notes.md` argues for a conclusion, SQs drawn from it would only look for support. `skeptic` adds SQs that look for evidence against it (row 1b, `flow-sls.md`)."," - **Start set bias:** snowballing only from `seeds.md` mostly finds more of the same. The start set also uses a database search string (row 2a), as Wohlin recommends a varied start set [1]."," ","-## Closing the loop","-At the end of the SLS, DSRM 1 checks step 0 against the literature and builds what step 0 could not: the motivation backed by the review (rows 6a–6d, `flow-dsrm.md`).","-"," ---"," "," ## References"," 1. Wohlin (2014). *Guidelines for Snowballing in Systematic Literature Studies*. Conference on Evaluation and Assessment in Software Engineering.","+2. Peffers et al. (2007). *A Design Science Research Methodology for Information Systems Research*. Journal of Management Information Systems 24(3)."]}],"originalFile":"# Kickstart\n\nThe pipeline starts from step 0: what I define before any literature or AI. It lives in `research/0-start/`, apart from the DSRM steps, because it is my input, not the output of any step.\n\n## Step 0: what I define\nFor the problem-centred route (`entry-points.md`):\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `observation.md` | The observation as a gap: what is true in one domain and not in another |\n| `design-problem.md` | What I want to solve, in Wieringa's template |\n| `RQ{n}.md` | What I want to find out: the question, its Shaw type, why the answer could be \"no\" |\n| `H{n}.md` | What I believe is different now, to be tested |\n| `stances.md` | Design stances: what is out of scope, and why |\n| `stakeholders.md` | Who is affected and their goals |\n| `claims.md` | What I believe the literature says: why it matters, why earlier attempts failed. Each claim with its source. |\n| `seeds.md` | The references I already know. Seeds for the start set (row 2a), not results. |\n\n| # | Who | Input | Output | Gate |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **0** | Me | — | `0-start/*.md`, all `provisional` | **Start.** Frozen when row 1a runs. |\n\n## Rules\n- **Step 0 is never edited after it is frozen.** Only `status` changes. If the SLS shows something in it is wrong, the corrected version is a new file with a new ID in `dsrm/1-problem/`, and the old one is `retired` (the ID rule, `dsrm/README.md`).\n- **`claims.md` and `seeds.md` are what the AI checks.** The other files are my intent; the AI attacks them (`skeptic`), but they change only if I change them.\n\n## Safeguards\nStep 0 already argues for a conclusion, so:\n- **Protocol bias:** SQs drawn only from my hypotheses would only look for support. `skeptic` adds SQs that look for evidence against them (row 1b, `flow-sls.md`).\n- **Start set bias:** snowballing only from `seeds.md` mostly finds more of the same. The start set also uses a database search string (row 2a), as Wohlin recommends a varied start set [1].\n\n## Closing the loop\nAt the end of the SLS, DSRM 1 checks step 0 against the literature and builds what step 0 could not: the motivation backed by the review (rows 6a–6d, `flow-dsrm.md`).\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. Wohlin (2014). *Guidelines for Snowballing in Systematic Literature Studies*. Conference on Evaluation and Assessment in Software Engineering.\n","userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e7436776-bebd-4580-9969-f3ef06637d01","session_id":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/garro/dev/faculty/Thesis/research/ai","sessionId":"f5479a1a-28cc-4c2d-bc70-ca6abee30b5e","version":"2.1.289","gitBranch":"main"}
