{"parentUuid":"e2e120e9-8d35-4dff-8030-cbb538807008","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfm4167DQKN8m8V88QCV1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're right, and that was my mistake. I built step 0 by sorting your existing draft into files, so everything you'd already written ended up there as if it were required. Step 0 is now just the observation. I've updated the docs to match.\n\n**New step 0 (`0-start/`):**\n\n| File | Required | Holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| `observation.md` | **yes** | The observation |\n| `seeds.md` | no | References you already know, used to seed the start set |\n| `notes.md` | no | Whatever you already think about the problem, as you wrote it |\n\nEverything else moved to **DSRM 1**, after the SLS. That's also where Peffers puts it, because the problem is built from knowledge of the literature, which you don't have yet at step 0. DSRM 1 now runs like this:\n- **6a:** `synthesizer` writes `motivation.md` from the gap. If `notes.md` exists, it also writes `revision.md`, checking each claim in your notes against the literature.\n- **6b:** `synthesizer` drafts `design-problem.md`, `RQ{n}.md`, `H{n}.md`, `stances.md` and `stakeholders.md`.\n- **6c:** `skeptic` attacks those drafts.\n- **6d:** You decide.\n\nSo you still decide the problem, the RQs and the rest. You do it in DSRM 1 at the 6d gate, with the AI's drafts in front of you, not alone at step 0.\n\n**Your existing draft isn't lost.** When we migrate:\n- 1.1 becomes `observation.md`.\n- Your 21 references become `seeds.md`.\n- The rest of your `dsrm/1-problem/step.md` goes into `notes.md` as it is, without splitting.\n\nThe AI then reuses your RQs, hypotheses and design problem as a starting point in 6b, and checks your literature claims in 6a.\n\nI updated `kickstart.md`, `flow-dsrm.md` (rows 6a–6d, and 7a now reads RQs only from `dsrm/1-problem/`), `entry-points.md`, `agents.md` and the index in `README.md`."}],"conta