{"parentUuid":"d54c847a-c0c3-46e6-8c81-6e14763321d7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfm4xGTqpTPHqcbTBFT8u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015nBH6aoMm85Drggk2SAhV4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"R_KC='Kitchenham, Charters (2007). *Guidelines for Performing Systematic Literature Reviews in Software Engineering*. Keele University and Durham University technical report.'\nR_W='Wohlin (2014). *Guidelines for Snowballing in Systematic Literature Studies*. Conference on Evaluation and Assessment in Software Engineering.'\nR_K='Keshav (2007). *How to Read a Paper*. Association for Computing Machinery Special Interest Group on Data Communication, Computer Communication Review 37(3).'\nR_WW='Webster, Watson (2002). *Analyzing the Past to Prepare for the Future: Writing a Literature Review*. Management Information Systems Quarterly 26(2).'\nR_P='Peffers et al. (2007). *A Design Science Research Methodology for Information Systems Research*. Journal of Management Information Systems 24(3).'\nR_WI='Wieringa (2014). *Design Science Methodology for Information Systems and Software Engineering*. Springer.'\nR_S='Shaw (2003). *Writing Good Software Engineering Research Papers*. International Conference on Software Engineering.'\nR_WO='Wohlin et al. (2012). *Experimentation in Software Engineering*. Springer.'\nR_GH='Gregor, Hevner (2013). *Positioning and Presenting Design Science Research for Maximum Impact*. Management Information Systems Quarterly 37(2).'\n\ncat > sls/1-protocol/step.md <<EOF\n# Step 1 — Protocol\n\n> **Inputs:** \\`0-start/\\` · **Next:** \\`2-search/\\`\n\n> Kitchenham and Charters [1]: define the review before running it.\n\n## Rules\n- The protocol is written and frozen before any search, so selection does not bend to what I hoped to find [1].\n- Every SQ is served by at least one field of the extraction form.\n- Any change after freezing is a change file with its reason.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/sls/1-protocol/\\`. Flow rows 1a–1c (\\`ai/flow-sls.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`need.md\\` | What the review is about; existing reviews and why they are not enough |\n| \\`SQ{n}.md\\` | One review question each |\n| \\`criteria.md\\` | Inclusion (I) and exclusion (X) criteria |\n| \\`quality.md\\` | Quality checklist (Q) |\n| \\`extraction-form.md\\` | Fields extracted from every paper |\n| \\`changes/{date}-{slug}.md\\` | One per change after freezing |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_KC\nEOF\n\ncat > sls/2-search/step.md <<EOF\n# Step 2 — Search (snowballing)\n\n> **Inputs:** \\`1-protocol/\\`, \\`0-start/seeds.md\\` · **Next:** \\`3-reading/\\` for each paper included\n\n> Wohlin [1]: start from a set of papers, then follow references backward and citations forward, round by round.\n\n## Rules\n- **Start set:** seeds plus a database search, so it is varied [1].\n- **Each round** examines only the papers included in the previous one.\n  - **Backward:** the reference list of each paper.\n  - **Forward:** the papers that cite it.\n- **Screening** applies only the criteria in \\`1-protocol/criteria.md\\`.\n- **Stop** when a round includes no new papers [1].\n- Papers get P IDs in the order they are first included. Never renumber.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/sls/2-search/\\`. Flow rows 2a–2d (\\`ai/flow-sls.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`search-string.md\\` | The database search and where it was run |\n| \\`R0.md\\` | The start set, screened |\n| \\`R{k}-backward.md\\`, \\`R{k}-forward.md\\` | One round in one direction, screened |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_W\nEOF\n\ncat > sls/3-reading/step.md <<EOF\n# Step 3 — Reading and extraction\n\n> **Inputs:** a paper included in \\`2-search/\\`, \\`1-protocol/quality.md\\`, \\`1-protocol/extraction-form.md\\` · **Next:** \\`4-synthesis/\\`\n\n> Keshav [1]: read in up to three passes, stopping when the paper has given what you need.\n> Kitchenham and Charters [2]: assess quality and extract the same fields from every paper.\n\n## Rules\n- Every extracted value and concept has a page.\n- Papers my argument depends on are read in full by me.\n\n## Produces\nTemplate in \\`ai/template/sls/3-reading/\\`. Flow rows 3a–3c (\\`ai/flow-sls.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`P{n}.md\\` | The record of one paper: five Cs, quality, extracted fields, concepts |\n| \\`P{n}.citations.md\\` | The citation check of that record |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_K\n2. $R_KC\nEOF\n\ncat > sls/4-synthesis/step.md <<EOF\n# Step 4 — Synthesis (concept matrix)\n\n> **Inputs:** accepted \\`3-reading/P*.md\\` · **Next:** \\`5-gap/\\`\n\n> Webster and Watson [1]: organize the review by concept, not by author.\n> - Rows = papers. Columns = concepts found in the papers.\n> - Mark a cell when the paper discusses that concept, with the page.\n\n## Rules\n- The matrix is generated from the concepts in each \\`P{n}.md\\` (\\`just views\\`), never written by hand.\n\n## Produces\nTemplate in \\`ai/template/sls/4-synthesis/\\`. Flow rows 4a–4b (\\`ai/flow-sls.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`C{n}.md\\` | One concept: definition, and what the papers say, compared |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_WW\nEOF\n\ncat > sls/5-gap/step.md <<EOF\n# Step 5 — Answers and gap\n\n> **Inputs:** accepted \\`4-synthesis/C*.md\\`, \\`1-protocol/SQ*.md\\` · **Next:** \\`dsrm/1-problem/\\`\n\n> Webster and Watson [1]: the gap is what the matrix shows nobody has done, or done only in part.\n> Kitchenham and Charters [2]: report what could have biased the review.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/sls/5-gap/\\`. Flow rows 5a–5c (\\`ai/flow-sls.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`SQ{n}.answer.md\\` | The answer to one review question |\n| \\`gap.md\\` | What no paper covers, or covers only in part |\n| \\`solutions.md\\` | Current solutions and how far they get |\n| \\`history.md\\` | Why it has not been done, or why earlier attempts failed |\n| \\`limitations.md\\` | What could have biased the review |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_WW\n2. $R_KC\nEOF\n\ncat > dsrm/1-problem/step.md <<EOF\n# Activity 1 — Problem identification and motivation\n\n> **Inputs:** \\`0-start/\\`, \\`sls/5-gap/\\` · **Next:** \\`2-objectives/\\`\n\n> Peffers [1]: define the research problem and justify the value of a solution, from knowledge of the state of the problem.\n\n## Rules\n- **Design problem, exactly one.** Template (Wieringa [2]): *Improve <a problem context> by <(re)designing an artifact> that satisfies <some requirements> in order to <help stakeholders achieve some goals>.*\n- **Stakeholders:** anyone affected by the problem or the artifact [2].\n- **Knowledge questions** use Shaw's types [3]. Each one could be answered \"no\".\n\n| Type [3] | Typical form [3] |\n|---|---|\n| Method or means of development | \"How can we do X?\" / \"What is a better way to do X?\" |\n| Method for analysis or evaluation | \"How can I evaluate the quality of X?\" |\n| Design, evaluation, or analysis of a particular instance | \"How good is Y?\" / \"What is property X of Y?\" |\n| Generalization or characterization | \"What, exactly, do we mean by X?\" / \"What are the important characteristics of X?\" |\n| Feasibility study or exploration | \"Does X even exist?\" / \"Is it possible to accomplish X at all?\" |\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/dsrm/1-problem/\\`. Flow rows 6a–6d (\\`ai/flow-dsrm.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`motivation.md\\` | Why it matters and why earlier attempts failed, backed by the review |\n| \\`revision.md\\` | Each claim in \\`0-start/notes.md\\` checked against the review |\n| \\`design-problem.md\\` | The design problem |\n| \\`RQ{n}.md\\` | One knowledge question each |\n| \\`H{n}.md\\` | One hypothesis each, tested by an RQ |\n| \\`stances.md\\` | What the thesis deliberately does not solve |\n| \\`stakeholders.md\\` | Who is affected and their goals |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_P\n2. $R_WI\n3. $R_S\nEOF\n\ncat > dsrm/2-objectives/step.md <<EOF\n# Activity 2 — Define the objectives for a solution\n\n> **Inputs:** accepted \\`1-problem/RQ*.md\\`, \\`sls/5-gap/solutions.md\\` · **Next:** \\`3-design/\\`\n\n> Peffers [1]: infer the objectives from the problem definition and from knowledge of what is possible and feasible.\n> - **Quantitative:** how a desirable solution would be better than current ones.\n> - **Qualitative:** how the artifact is expected to support solutions to problems not addressed before.\n\n## Rules\n- Rigor aid (mine, not Peffers'): every objective says how it will be checked, so step 5 has something to compare against.\n- Objectives, metrics and baselines are frozen before any result is seen.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/dsrm/2-objectives/\\`. Flow rows 7a–7c (\\`ai/flow-dsrm.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`O{n}.md\\` | One objective: what it traces to, kind, how it is checked, validation type |\n| \\`metrics.md\\` | What is measured and how |\n| \\`baselines.md\\` | What the artifact is compared with, and why that is fair |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_P\nEOF\n\ncat > dsrm/3-design/step.md <<EOF\n# Activity 3 — Design and development\n\n> **Inputs:** accepted \\`2-objectives/O*.md\\`, \\`sls/4-synthesis/C*.md\\` · **Next:** \\`4-demonstration/\\`\n\n> Peffers [1]: create the artifact, deciding its functionality and architecture.\n\n## Rules\n- Build the cheapest layer first. Each layer is demonstrated and evaluated before the next.\n\n| Layer | |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Paper design |\n| 2 | Executable model / simulator |\n| 3 | Formal specification (optional) |\n| 4 | Hardware prototype (optional) |\n\n- The layer itself lives in \\`artifact/L{n}/\\`; its form for each layer is in \\`ai/agents.md\\`.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/dsrm/3-design/\\`. Flow rows 8a–8d (\\`ai/flow-dsrm.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`DEC{n}.options.md\\` | The options for one design decision |\n| \\`DEC{n}.md\\` | The option chosen and why |\n| \\`L{n}.md\\` | What was built in one layer, and its checks |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_P\nEOF\n\ncat > dsrm/4-demonstration/step.md <<EOF\n# Activity 4 — Demonstration\n\n> **Inputs:** accepted \\`3-design/L{n}.md\\`, \\`2-objectives/O*.md\\` · **Next:** \\`5-evaluation/\\`\n\n> Peffers [1]: show that the artifact solves one or more instances of the problem, through experimentation, simulation, case study, proof, or another fitting activity.\n\n## Rules\n- A demonstration answers \"does it work on this case?\", not \"how well?\". How well is step 5.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/dsrm/4-demonstration/\\`. Flow rows 9a–9d (\\`ai/flow-dsrm.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`D{n}.md\\` | One planned demonstration: instance, layer, activity, objectives fed |\n| \\`D{n}-run{k}.md\\` | One run of it, and whether it solved the instance |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. $R_P\nEOF\n\ncat > dsrm/5-evaluation/step.md <<EOF\n# Activity 5 — Evaluation\n\n> **Inputs:** accepted \\`2-objectives/\\`, \\`4-demonstration/D*-run*.md\\` · **Next:** \\`6-communicate/\\`, or iterate back to \\`2-objectives/\\` or \\`3-design/\\` [1]\n\n> Peffers [1]: observe and measure how well the artifact supports a solution, by comparing the objectives with the results observed in the demonstration.\n\n## Rules\n- Each objective's validation type (Shaw [2]: analysis, evaluation, experience, example, persuasion) is set in its \\`O{n}.md\\`.\n- Every evaluation states where the next iteration goes.\n- Threats to validity use the four categories of Wohlin et al. [3]: conclusion, internal, construct, external.\n\n## Produces\nTemplates in \\`ai/template/dsrm/5-evaluation/\\`. Flow rows 10a–10c (\\`ai/flow-dsrm.md\\`).\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| \\`E{n}.md\\` | One evaluation: measurements, met or not, what comes next |\n| \\`T{n}.md\\` | One threat to validity and what I did about it