{"parentUuid":"fd466440-8b8b-42d3-b560-5c266ac85f08","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4d3d5818-f443-4d15-bbf5-b6c51e69b9ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ScTvJHN4irEqS8qdxSzKRr","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nid: observation\nrow: 0\nby: me\nstatus: provisional\ninputs: []\n---\n# Observation\n\nMessage abstractions (OOP, actors) are used in languages, agents and networks, but not in operating systems, hardware and low-level programming.\n\n## How I noticed it\nWhile coding and studying in different situations:\n- While coding parallel algorithms at a C/Rust/Assembly Level i noticed that the computers still think imperativly while we, programmers, work/think/design in object or message oriented paradigms\n- While writing an interpreter in Java i noticed that most interpreters job is reading text and translating that into a keyword instead of dispatching objects behaviours or even function calls, which means your language is defined into primitives which are not the system and then it cannot define itself, unlike smalltalk\n- While seeing how microservices work and their difference with low level computing i noticed that while network systems are prepared to work via messages isolating their state our machines in a low level still expose their state as global/shared at a hardware level while knowing the consequences of it\n M research/0-start/observation.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d4227073-2472-43b9-b752-29847b132c59","timestamp":"2026-10-06T14:03:23.569Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nid: observation\nrow: 0\nby: me\nstatus: provisional\ninputs: []\n---\n# Observation\n\nMessage abstractions (OOP, actors) are used in languages, agents and networks, but not in operating systems, hardware and low-level programming.\n\n## How I noticed it\nWhile coding and studying in different situations:\n- While coding parallel algorithms at a C/Rust/Assembly Level i noticed that the computers still think imperativly while we, 